(Bug #58's worst symptom, and the reason it is a memory-safety bug rather
 than a wrong answer. `Set <buffer> to <property>` re-read the name's type
 off the initializer, saw an argument read, and re-labelled a declared
 buffer as text - and once the name was no longer a buffer the assignment
 stopped copying bytes into the buffer struct and stored the raw argv
 pointer straight over it.

 From there every buffer operation was aimed at the process's own argv
 block. `capacity` read the argument's first eight bytes as an integer
 (8100956900724599905 for "alphaalp"), so the bounds check compared against
 a number no position could exceed and passed everything: `Set byte 1 of
 assigned` wrote into the *next* argument's storage, and a large position
 segfaulted (139) on origin/main.

 The 8-byte declaration is deliberate: the write below is far outside it,
 so a buffer that kept its own capacity must refuse the write and leave
 both the argument block and the program intact.)

a buffer called assigned is 8 bytes in size.
Set assigned to arguments's first.
Print assigned's type.
Print assigned's capacity.
Print assigned's size.

Set byte 100000000 of assigned to 'X'.
On error print "out-of-bounds write refused".

Set byte 1 of assigned to 'X'.
Print assigned.
Print arguments's first.
Print arguments's second.
Print "survived".
